                <h2>第 1 步：先统一“什么叫能跑，什么叫能卖”</h2>
<p>Meta 素材复盘最常见的问题，是把点击率高误判成转化好。素材“能跑”只说明它能获得曝光、产生点击并顺利消耗预算；“能卖”则要求用户完成购买、注册或有效留资，并且收入能够覆盖获客成本。</p>
<p>复盘前，先在广告组或表格中定义四个口径：</p>
<ul>
<li>转化目标：购买、订阅、留资还是激活；</li>
<li>归因窗口：按团队当前使用的 Meta 归因规则统一记录；</li>
<li>观察周期：至少覆盖一个完整的销售或转化周期，避免只看当天数据；</li>
<li>盈亏标准：明确可接受 CPA、目标 ROAS 或单客贡献利润。</li>
</ul>
<p>不同业务的付费周期差异很大。电商应同时关注退款率和实际收款，工具类 App 应关注试用后的付费率，游戏则应结合留存、付费率与 LTV 判断，不能仅用 CTR 得出结论。</p>
<h2>第 2 步：给每条素材建立可追踪标签</h2>
<p>素材名称建议包含：卖点、表达形式、受众场景和版本。例如“省时—痛点对比—新用户—V2”。</p>
<p>每次测试只改变一个主要变量，如卖点不变，只替换开头画面；或形式不变，只调整文案。否则即使结果变好，也无法判断具体原因。</p>
<p>同时记录以下信息：</p>
<ol>
<li>素材来源与制作日期；</li>
<li>核心卖点及用户痛点；</li>
<li>视频长度、首屏画面和 CTA；</li>
<li>落地页版本；</li>
<li>投放受众、预算和优化事件；</li>
<li>异常情况，如促销变化、断货或追踪故障。</li>
</ol>
<p>这一步能让复盘从“大家凭印象讨论”变成基于同一证据做判断。</p>
<h2>第 3 步：用两张表分别判断“能跑”和“能卖”</h2>
<h3>流量层：判断素材是否具备跑量能力</h3>
<p>重点查看展示份额、消耗速度、CPM、CTR、落地页浏览率和加购或试用启动率。若曝光充足但点击率弱，通常要检查首屏、受众匹配和卖点表达；若点击率正常但转化链路中断，则不能继续归因于素材。</p>
<h3>销售层：判断流量是否产生商业价值</h3>
<p>重点查看转化量、有效转化率、CPA、实际 ROAS、贡献利润和退款率。可使用以下思路：</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>单次贡献利润 = 实收金额－商品成本－履约成本－支付及渠道费用－广告成本</strong>；</li>
<li><strong>LTV/CAC</strong> 用于评估长期获客质量；</li>
<li>对留资业务，应继续区分“表单提交”与“有效线索”，避免用低质量线索制造虚假繁荣。</li>
</ul>
<p>只有跑量表和销售表同时通过，才进入放量候选池。能跑但不能卖的素材，应优先检查承诺与落地页是否一致，而不是立即否定创意。</p>
<h2>第 4 步：按问题类型做素材诊断</h2>
<p>复盘时按顺序追问：</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>用户是否停下来看？</strong> CTR 低时，调整首屏冲突点、字幕可读性和利益点表达。</li>
<li><strong>点击后是否继续？</strong> 落地页浏览率低时，检查加载速度、页面一致性及移动端体验。</li>
<li><strong>用户为何没有转化？</strong> 对比不同卖点、异议处理、信任证据和 CTA，定位流失环节。</li>
<li><strong>转化是否真实？</strong> 检查重复转化、无效点击、归因重复、退款和取消订单。</li>
<li><strong>销售质量如何？</strong> 对高 CPA 素材继续追踪留存、复购和长期利润，而不是只看首单。</li>
</ol>
<p>某工具类 App 曾发现，一条视频 CTR 较高，但试用注册 CPA 超过目标。排查发现素材强调“免费生成”，落地页却突出企业版价格，用户预期不一致。团队将首屏改为功能演示，并统一落地页承诺后，注册质量才明显改善。这个案例说明，高点击并不等于高转化意愿。</p>
<h2>第 5 步：执行“保留、迭代、停投、放量”决策</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>保留</strong>：消耗稳定，转化质量达标，有扩大空间；</li>
<li><strong>迭代</strong>：流量表现好，但销售端存在明确问题；</li>
<li><strong>停投</strong>：样本与观察周期充分，仍无法达到盈亏标准；</li>
<li><strong>放量</strong>：销售指标稳定后，再逐步增加预算并同步复制素材结构。</li>
</ul>
<p>放量不要只看 CPA 下降。更稳妥的做法是按预算阶梯逐步调整，同时监控边际 CPA、转化率和退款率。若扩大预算后流量质量下降，应暂停扩量并检查受众扩张。同一广告组中的新素材也要接受独立评估，避免头部素材掩盖全部效果。</p>
<h2>第 6 步：用固定模板完成周度复盘</h2>
<p>每条素材只需回答六个问题：</p>
<blockquote>
<ol>
<li>它服务什么人群和卖点？  </li>
<li>是否达到有效观察条件？  </li>
<li>流量层哪里最好、哪里最差？  </li>
<li>销售层是否达到盈亏标准？  </li>
<li>问题发生在素材、落地页还是追踪环节？  </li>
<li>下一步是保留、迭代、停投还是放量？</li>
</ol>
</blockquote>
<p>最终把素材分成四类：能跑不能卖、能卖不能跑、既跑又卖、两者都不行。对应动作分别是修正转化链路、争取更多曝光、优先扩量、及时止损。这样复盘的结果才能真正指导投放，而不是只留下几条“CTR 很高”的截图。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Step 1: Define What “Scaleable” and “Sales-Producing” Mean</h2>
<p>The most common mistake in Meta creative reviews is treating a high click-through rate as proof of conversion performance. A creative that is “scaleable” can generate impressions, clicks, and stable delivery. A creative that “sells” should drive purchases, subscriptions, qualified leads, or activations that generate enough revenue to offset acquisition costs.</p>
<p>Before reviewing results, align four items:</p>
<ul>
<li>Conversion objective: purchase, subscription, lead, or activation;</li>
<li>Attribution window: use the same Meta attribution setup across the review;</li>
<li>Observation period: cover a meaningful sales or conversion cycle;</li>
<li>Break-even criteria: define the target CPA, ROAS, or contribution margin.</li>
</ul>
<p>Payment behavior varies by business. E-commerce teams should monitor refunds and collected revenue, while tool apps should examine trial-to-paid conversion, and gaming campaigns should evaluate retention, paying-user rate, and LTV. CTR alone is not a reliable conclusion.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Create a Traceable Tagging System</h2>
<p>Name each asset with the audience or use case, key selling point, creative format, and version, such as <code>time-saving–pain-point-demo–new-users–V2</code>.</p>
<p>Change only one major variable in each test. Keep the message constant when testing a new opening shot, or keep the format constant when testing different copy. Otherwise, the team cannot determine which change caused the result.</p>
<p>Also record the source, production date, core proposition, opening frame, length, CTA, landing-page version, audience, budget, optimization event, and any disruptions such as stock shortages or tracking issues.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Separate Delivery Performance from Sales Performance</h2>
<h3>Delivery layer</h3>
<p>Review impression share, spending pace, CPM, CTR, landing-page views, and cart or trial starts. Low CTR with adequate delivery often points to a weak opening frame, poor audience-message fit, or an unclear benefit. Strong clicks followed by weak landing-page activity suggest an issue outside the ad itself.</p>
<h3>Sales layer</h3>
<p>Review conversion volume, qualified conversion rate, CPA, realized ROAS, contribution profit, and refunds. A useful calculation is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Contribution profit per order = collected revenue − product cost − fulfillment cost − payment and channel fees − ad cost</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Use LTV/CAC when assessing long-term acquisition quality. For lead-generation campaigns, separate form submissions from qualified leads to prevent volume from masking poor lead quality.</p>
<p>A creative should enter the scaling pool only when both layers pass the agreed thresholds. If delivery is strong but sales are weak, first check whether the ad promise matches the landing-page offer.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Diagnose the Leakage Point</h2>
<p>Ask the following questions in order:</p>
<ol>
<li>Did the audience stop and watch? If CTR is weak, revise the opening hook, captions, and benefit hierarchy.</li>
<li>Did visitors continue after the click? Check page speed, mobile experience, and message continuity.</li>
<li>Why did they not convert? Compare the proposition, objection handling, trust signals, and CTA.</li>
<li>Are the conversions genuine? Review duplicates, invalid traffic, attribution errors, cancellations, and refunds.</li>
<li>Is the sales quality acceptable? Track retention, repeat purchases, and long-term profit after the first conversion.</li>
</ol>
<p>Consider an anonymized tool app whose video generated a high CTR but produced trial registrations above the target CPA. The creative promised free generation, while the landing page emphasized enterprise pricing. After the team demonstrated the product in the opening frame and aligned the landing-page promise, registration quality improved. The example shows that strong curiosity does not necessarily equal purchase intent.</p>
<h2>Step 5: Apply Four Clear Decisions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep</strong>: delivery is stable, conversion quality meets the break-even standard, and there is room to scale;</li>
<li><strong>Iterate</strong>: delivery works, but a specific sales-side issue must be corrected;</li>
<li><strong>Stop</strong>: sufficient evidence has been collected and the asset cannot meet the profit threshold;</li>
<li><strong>Scale</strong>: sales metrics remain stable while budget increases.</li>
</ul>
<p>Scale budgets in controlled steps and monitor marginal CPA, conversion rate, and refunds. If quality falls as delivery expands, pause and investigate audience expansion. Evaluate every new creative independently; a strong asset in the same ad set should not hide weak performers.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Use a Weekly Review Template</h2>
<p>For each creative, answer six questions:</p>
<blockquote>
<ol>
<li>Which audience and proposition does it target?  </li>
<li>Has it received a fair and sufficient observation period?  </li>
<li>What performed best or worst at the delivery level?  </li>
<li>Does it meet the break-even criteria for sales?  </li>
<li>Is the problem in the creative, landing page, offer, or tracking?  </li>
<li>Should the next action be keep, iterate, stop, or scale?</li>
</ol>
</blockquote>
<p>Organize the final list into four groups: scaleable but not converting, converting but not scaleable, scaleable and converting, and neither. Each group requires a different action—fix the conversion path, improve delivery, prioritize scaling, or stop the asset. This turns creative review into an operational decision system rather than a discussion based on screenshots and impressions.</p>
